<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gillo Pontecorvo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gillo Pontecorvo (1919–2006) was an Italian film director and former anti-fascist partisan. His film The Battle of Algiers (1966), made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> with largely non-professional actors, won the Golden Lion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and became one of the most influential political films ever made. His other works include Kapò (1960) and Burn! (1969), starring Marlon Brando.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gillo Pontecorvo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> on 19 November 1919 into a wealthy secular Italian Jewish family. His brothers included Bruno, a nuclear physicist who defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1950, and Guido, a geneticist who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He studied chemistry at the University of Pisa but dropped out after passing two exams, and there he first met leftist students and professors. In 1938, faced with growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>, he followed Bruno to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he worked in journalism and as a tennis instructor and began making short documentaries. He assisted the directors Joris Ivens and Yves Allégret and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-stravinsky">Igor Stravinsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a>.</p><p>In 1941 Pontecorvo joined the Italian Communist Party. He went to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to help organise anti-fascist partisans and, under the pseudonym Barnaba, led the Resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1943 until 1945. From 1948 to 1950 he co-edited the communist weekly Pattuglia. He broke with the party in 1956 after the Soviet intervention against the Hungarian uprising, though he did not renounce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The catalyst for his turn to film was Roberto Rossellini's Paisà (1946). He bought a 16mm camera and made documentaries, beginning with Missione Timiriazev in 1953. His first full-length film, La grande strada azzurra (1957), about a fisherman off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, won a prize at the Karlovy Vary International Film Festival. Kapò (1960), a drama set in a Nazi death camp, was nominated for an Oscar for Best Foreign-Language Film. He spent months, sometimes years, researching the social situations he portrayed.</p><p>The Battle of Algiers (1966) portrays the Algerian resistance in the Algerian War in a neorealist style, with newsreel-style footage and almost entirely non-professional actors; the part of Djafar was played by the FLN leader Yacef Saadi. It won the Golden Lion at the 27th Venice Film Festival and earned Pontecorvo Oscar nominations for direction and screenplay. It has been used as a training video by revolutionary groups and by military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance. Burn! (1969), with Marlon Brando, concerns a fictional slave revolt in the Lesser Antilles, and Ogro (1979) concerns Basque separatists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under Franco. He directed the Venice Film Festival from 1992 to 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pontecorvo died of congestive heart failure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 12 October 2006, aged 86. He is associated with the political cinema of the 1960s and 1970s, and the semi-documentary style of The Battle of Algiers influenced later filmmakers. It remains popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> as a memory of the struggle for independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gillo Pontecorvo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frantz Fanon, who worked as a psychiatrist at Blida, theorised it in The Wretched of the Earth (1961), and <b>Gillo Pontecorvo</b>’s film The Battle of Algiers (1966) made it known worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Pisa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gillo Pontecorvo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5639 3809h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5639 3809h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
